// ============================================================================
// Resolution · OVERLOAD SETS  (the signature selected vs the body that runs)
//
// 44.3% of measured resolved call targets are BODILESS, and 99.6% of those are
// ambient. That number is not an anomaly to be fixed — it is what a call into a
// typed library IS. But it means a call chain that stops at whatever the resolver
// selected stops at a DECLARATION, and a consumer asking "what code runs" gets a row
// with no body behind it.
//
// Java has nothing like this. A Java call resolves to a method that either has a
// body or is abstract, and abstract-to-concrete is virtual dispatch. Here there are
// three distinct reasons a target has no body, and they need three different answers:
//
//   NO_BODY_OVERLOAD   an overload signature. The body EXISTS, in the same file, as
//                      the IMPLEMENTATION row of the same declarationGroupKey. This
//                      file re-points to it. Purely mechanical, no inference.
//   NO_BODY_INTERFACE  an interface method signature. The body is in some
//                      implementing class, reached by dispatch — a different
//                      question, answered in callee-resolution.dl.
//   NO_BODY_AMBIENT    a `declare`d signature. There IS no body in any staged IR;
//                      the implementation is native or in JavaScript that was never
//                      typed. An honest terminal, and the correct end of a chain.
//
// Conflating the three is how a call graph either loses the overload body it had all
// along, or invents a body for `fs.readFile` that does not exist in any TypeScript.
//
// ── STRATIFICATION ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Reads projections and overload.dl's output. Feeds only the call-edge layer, never
// expr_type or expr_call_candidate, so it is a leaf on the resolution side and its
// negations cannot reach back into the fixpoint.
// ============================================================================

// ── group_implementation(GroupKey, MethodHash) ──────────────────────────────
// The one row of an overload set that has a body.
group_implementation(g, m) :- method_group("client", g, m),
    method_signature_role("client", "IMPLEMENTATION", _, _, m).
group_implementation(g, m) :- method_group("lib", g, m),
    method_signature_role("lib", "IMPLEMENTATION", _, _, m).

// ── method_body_presence(MethodHash, BodyPresence) ──────────────────────────
method_body_presence(m, bp) :- method_signature_role("client", _, _, bp, m).
method_body_presence(m, bp) :- method_signature_role("lib", _, _, bp, m).

body_presence_has_body("HAS_BODY").
// The three bodiless reasons, enumerated so a new one shows up as an unclassified
// target rather than silently joining whichever bucket a literal happened to match.
body_presence_is_overload("NO_BODY_OVERLOAD").
body_presence_is_signature("NO_BODY_INTERFACE").
body_presence_is_signature("NO_BODY_ABSTRACT").
body_presence_is_ambient("NO_BODY_AMBIENT").

// ── call_runs_method(CallExprHash, MethodHash) ──────────────────────────────
// What actually executes. Identity where the resolved target has a body; the
// group's implementation where it is an overload signature.
//
// EXPORTED, per call site. call_runs_edge below is this relation rolled up to the
// caller METHOD, which is what a reachability walk wants and which cannot answer
// "at THIS site, what runs". A consumer that has a position, and the runtime
// oracle is one, had no way to ask: it read call_edges, saw the SIGNATURE the
// compiler selected, and charged the engine with missing the body. Measured on
// immer 061c242, 366 executed production sites: 7 of the 17 OUTSIDE rows were
// this, six of them one `each` whose signature is common.ts:84 and whose
// implementation is common.ts:89.
call_runs_method(ce, m) :- expr_resolves_to_method(ce, m),
    method_body_presence(m, bp),
    body_presence_has_body(bp).
call_runs_method(ce, impl) :- expr_resolves_to_method(ce, m),
    method_body_presence(m, bp),
    body_presence_is_overload(bp),
    method_group_of(m, g),
    group_implementation(g, impl).

// ── call_target_is_ambient(CallExprHash) ────────────────────────────────────
// The chain ends here and that is the right answer. Classified rather than left as a
// gap, because a report that counts `fs.readFile` as an unresolved call site is
// wrong about the program, not merely pessimistic about it.
call_target_is_ambient(ce) :- expr_resolves_to_method(ce, m),
    method_body_presence(m, bp),
    body_presence_is_ambient(bp).

// ── call_target_is_signature(CallExprHash) ──────────────────────────────────
// Resolved to an interface or abstract declaration. The body is reachable only
// through dispatch, so a consumer that wants the implementation must read the
// multi_inferred fan rather than this edge.
call_target_is_signature(ce) :- expr_resolves_to_method(ce, m),
    method_body_presence(m, bp),
    body_presence_is_signature(bp).

// ── method_is_client_body(MethodHash) — a chain can continue through it ─────
// A target worth expanding: client code, with a body. This is the frontier for
// reachability and change-impact, and the reason a boundary_lib edge is marked and
// not followed.
method_is_client_body(m) :- method_signature_role("client", _, _, bp, m),
    body_presence_has_body(bp).
